ries these funds  may be forced to default or borrow money  to pay out their beneficiaries and given  that russian interest rates are as high  as credit cards right now you definitely  don't want to be borrowing money clearly  even though the average russian may not  directly have stock holdings a stock  market crash would be pretty painful for  them as well  at the end of the day the west doesn't  agree with putin's invasion of ukraine  and this has caused them to take severe  action 100 years ago the west may have  entered into a war against russia or  entered into an arms race against russia  but today governments are trying to  address the situation diplomatically  instead of fighting a deadly war or  building up weapons of mass destruction  the west is trying to get russia to back  off by threatening their economy and  with the biggest countries and companies  on board it seems to be pretty effective  in causing economic damage however it's  unclear if this will actually lead to  russia backing down here's the thing  even if they did back down putin has  permanently burned a lot of bridges by  deciding to invade so it's unlikely that  their economy will just return to normal  if they leave ukraine considering this  if the russian economy is screwed either  way why not make a statement on your way  out and it looks like that's what putin  has chosen to do  looking forward it's unlikely that the  russian economy will completely collapse  or enter hyperinflation though those do  have non-negligible probabilities the  more likely scenario is that russia  falls down another tier amongst world  powers economically with the everyday  russian bearing most of the fallout  while that's quite a shame hopefully all  these efforts make it substantially  harder for putin to invade yet another  country but only time will tell do you  guys think the russian economy is crude  comment that down below  also drop a like if you feel bad for the  everyday russian and of course consi